Pass inline dispatch arguments through one mapped ring, not a staging upload each

Every Arg::inline_bytes argument (meta counts, 3x3 matrices, LUT ranges;
72 call sites under engine/src/pipeline) became upload(): a pooled device
buffer, a memcpy into the staging buffer, then a vkCmdCopyBuffer in its own
submit followed by vkQueueWaitIdle, all before the dispatch's own submit
and wait. Most dispatches carry one or two such arguments, so a render
drained the queue two or three times per kernel launch.

The backend now creates one 64 KiB host-visible storage buffer at
initialisation, mapped for its life (preferring HOST_COHERENT, and
DEVICE_LOCAL where the device is unified memory, as Adreno is). Under the
dispatch mutex each inline argument is copied into it at an offset aligned
to minStorageBufferOffsetAlignment and bound with that offset and its own
size. The ring is rewritten from the start on every dispatch, which is safe
for the same reason the descriptor pool can be reset there (patch 0012):
dispatch holds dispatch_mutex_ until submit_sync has waited for the queue
to go idle. Host writes are flushed when the memory is not coherent and are
made visible by the queue submission itself.

If the device has no suitable memory type, the ring cannot be created, a
dispatch's inline bytes would not fit, or SPEKTRAFILM_VULKAN_INLINE_RING=0
is set, the old staging upload is used, so nothing fails for lack of it.

Output: byte-identical. Kernels receive the same bytes in a binding of the
same size (patch 0010 already binds requested sizes); only where those bytes
live and how they get there changes.

